## a. IPv4

**IPv4 (Internet Protocol version 4)** is a common system for assigning IP addresses to devices.

An IPv4 address has **4 numbers separated by dots**.

Example:

```text
192.168.1.10
```

Each number can be from **0 to 255**.

```text
192 . 168 . 1 . 10
 ↓     ↓    ↓    ↓
 1     2    3    4
```

IPv4 uses **32 bits**.

Example:

> Your laptop may have an IPv4 address such as `192.168.1.10` inside your home network.

**Easy idea:**

> **IPv4 = 32-bit IP address written as four numbers.**

---

## b. IPv6

**IPv6 (Internet Protocol version 6)** was developed to provide a much larger number of IP addresses.

IPv6 uses **128 bits**.

Example:

```text
2001:0db8:85a3:0000:0000:8a2e:0370:7334
```

IPv6 uses **hexadecimal numbers separated by colons**.

Example:

```text
IPv4
192.168.1.10

IPv6
2001:db8:85a3::8a2e:370:7334
```

Because IPv6 uses 128 bits, it provides a **much larger address space** than IPv4.

**Easy idea:**

> **IPv6 = 128-bit IP addressing system with a huge address space.**

